The '''Republic of Guajicutea (1801–1812)''' covers the period from the end of the [[Volisanian Civil War]] through the [[Invasion of Guajicutea]] by the [[Northern Confederation]] in 1812. The [[Patrimonio Diversidad]] (PD) movement, which had gained a plurality in the senate by 1801, enacted constitutional reforms that extended citizenship to all residents regardless of race, replaced the senate with a popularly elected representative assembly, and established a separately elected executive presidency. The PD government, under President [[Mateo Kurí]], severed the republic's alliance with [[Lacashe]] and withdrew from the [[Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League]], which dissolved in 1807. The [[National Republicanismo]] (NR) movement split into a reformist business faction, the [[Asociación Nuevo Guaji]] (ANG), and a radical exile faction that sought foreign support for the overthrow of the PD government. In 1812, the [[Northern Confederation]], having declared independence from [[Volisania]] in 1808, dispatched a naval squadron to support a coup led by NR exile [[Ramón Cifuentes]]. The two-month invasion resulted in the overthrow of the elected government, the arrest of President Kurí, and the placement of Guajicutea under Northern Confederation colonial rule.

== Constitutional reform (1801–1803) ==

The end of the Volisanian Civil War in 1801 enabled the PD to pursue its legislative programme. The PD had been gaining seats in the senate since the 1770s and had narrowed the margin of defeat on three citizenship bills (1786, 1791, 1796). By 1801, the party held a plurality. The remaining NR senators, reduced by electoral losses and the defection of moderate elements, were unable to block the reforms.

The Constitutional Amendment of 1803 enacted three changes:

* '''Universal citizenship''': All residents of Guajicutea — Sartan, Guaji, Kaftian, and Bayani — were granted full citizenship and political rights, regardless of ethnic origin or property ownership. The property qualification for suffrage, already removed for men in 1768, was extended to all adult male citizens. Women remained excluded.
* '''Representative assembly''': The senate, which had consisted of two senators per distrito regardless of population, was replaced by the '''Asamblea Nacional''', a representative assembly whose seats were allocated based on population. Districtos with larger populations received more seats, shifting political representation toward the more densely populated rural and urban areas where the PD's base was concentrated.
* '''Separate executive''': The office of Cónsul, elected by the senate, was replaced by a '''Presidente''' (president), directly elected by all citizens, serving as head of a separate executive branch. The president's term was set at four years.

The amendment was ratified in 1803 after a year of debate. The NR minority opposed all three changes, arguing that the restructuring would destabilise the republic's institutions. The ANG, which had not yet formally separated from the NR, supported the enfranchisement provision but opposed the assembly restructuring and the direct executive.

== Presidency of Mateo Kurí (1804–1812) ==

The first direct presidential election was held in 1804. The PD candidate, [[Mateo Kurí]], was elected by a wide margin. Kurí, a Guaji orator and organiser associated with the PD's parish network infrastructure, became the first Guaji to serve as head of state and the first president elected under the new constitution.

Kurí's presidency implemented the PD's domestic programme: Kaftian and Bayani voters were registered under the new citizenship provisions, the Asamblea Nacional was seated with proportional representation, and the government invested in secular education and infrastructure. The Church, stripped of its educational authority in 1784, retained its religious role but no longer controlled public schooling.

The Kurí government enacted the PD's anti-slavery foreign policy, a core element of the movement's platform since its founding by [[Esteban Makurí]]. The republic severed its alliance with [[Lacashe]], whose plantation economy continued to rely on chattel slavery. The severance ended a diplomatic relationship that had been the basis of Guajicutean foreign policy since the republic's founding in 1723.

== Dissolution of the Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League (1807) ==

The severance of relations with Lacashe affected the [[Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League]], founded in 1764 as a collective security alliance. Guajicutea and Lacashe had been the league's two founding members, and the anti-piracy cooperation that constituted the league's primary function depended on their participation. The league was formally dissolved in 1807.

The dissolution left the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} without a regional security framework for the first time in over four decades. Piracy increased in the absence of coordinated patrols.

== Political realignment ==

The constitutional reforms and the PD government produced a realignment of Guajicutean politics. The [[National Republicanismo]] (NR) movement, which had been the dominant ideology of the republic's first decades, split into three factions:

* '''[[Asociación Nuevo Guaji]] (ANG)''': A reformist faction of the NR, guided by business and merchant interests. The ANG accepted the constitutional reforms and competed in elections, gaining seats in the Asamblea Nacional. The ANG's platform was similar to the PD's on economic matters but advocated for a non-military constabulary (a civilian police force separate from the military) and non-religious secular education.
* '''Old NR''': The rump of the NR movement, which continued to oppose the reforms. The old NR retained a small presence in the Asamblea Nacional but lost electoral support over the period.
* '''NR exiles''': Radical elements of the NR who refused to accept the constitutional reforms or the PD government. These exiles left Guajicutea and sought support from the [[Northern Confederation]] for a coup to overthrow the PD government.

The ANG's participation in elections introduced a third party to a political landscape previously defined by the NR-PD binary since 1768.

== Northern Confederation and the coup plot (1808–1812) ==

The [[Northern Confederation]] declared independence from [[Volisania]] in 1808, formalised by the Treaty of Daramazán in 1809. The Confederation, a union of Baliscan republics including [[Hesperia]], Cerdanya, and [[Bayara]], possessed naval and military capacity.

The NR exiles, led by [[Ramón Cifuentes]], a former NR senator and landowner, travelled to the Northern Confederation to seek support for a coup against the PD government. The exiles argued that the PD government was illegitimate, that the constitutional reforms had been imposed without consensus, and that the rupture with Lacashe had isolated Guajicutea diplomatically. The state of [[Bayara]] within the Northern Confederation had commercial interests in the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} affected by the PD government's policies. Bayara's commercial sector regarded the PD's anti-slavery stance and the dissolution of the league as impediments to trade.

The Northern Confederation agreed to support a coup. In 1812, a naval squadron was dispatched to the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} to assist Cifuentes and his supporters.

== Invasion of Guajicutea (1812) ==

The [[Invasion of Guajicutea]] began in 1812 when the Northern Confederation naval squadron, carrying Cifuentes and his supporters, arrived in Guajicutean waters. The operation was primarily advocated by the state of [[Bayara]] within the Northern Confederation and was contentious within the Confederation, with some member states opposing the intervention.

The invasion lasted two months. Northern Confederation forces, supported by NR sympathisers on the ground, engaged Guajicutean military units loyal to the government. The Guajicutean forces, affected by reduced military spending under the PD government and the absence of the league's naval cooperation, were unable to repel the invasion.

President [[Mateo Kurí]] was arrested and the Asamblea Nacional was dissolved. Guajicutea was placed under Northern Confederation colonial rule. The invasion was regarded by regional observers as a demonstration of the Northern Confederation's military capacity and its willingness to project power beyond its territory.

== Aftermath ==

Following the invasion, the Northern Confederation dissolved the Asamblea Nacional and held re-elections administered by Northern Confederation occupation forces. A new political party, the [[Partido Unidad Guajicuteana]], was formed to support the occupation government, with a platform endorsing Northern Confederation authority.

Internal rebellions against the occupation occurred in the years following the 1812 invasion, concentrated in the rural districtos. The rebellions were conducted by supporters of the PD government and opponents of foreign rule.

== International reactions ==

* '''[[Lacashe]]''': Supported the occupation. The PD government's severance of relations had damaged Lacashe's diplomatic position, and the overthrow of the PD government was welcomed by Lacashe's planter elite.
* '''[[Tapuya]]''': Newly independent from Volisania in 1811, Tapuya supported the occupation, regarding the Northern Confederation's expansion as a stabilising presence in the region.
* '''[[Asikyira]]''': Opposed the occupation. Asikyira, a founding member of the Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League alongside Guajicutea, withdrew its emissary from Guajicutea.
* '''[[Balboa|Viceroyalty of Balboa]]''': Remained aligned with [[Volisania]] and opposed the invasion, regarding the Northern Confederation's expansion as a threat to the Volisanian colonial order.

